#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3833c1-4ec5-5681-ae69-6a9cceece871" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Slope and Y-Intercept From the Equation of a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "95fc8e7d-776c-5d09-9140-a83dc575ac79" :question/math-academy-id "e-12851" :question/problem "What are the slope and $y$-intercept of the line $y=2x-9?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is in slope-intercept form $y={\\color{red}{m}}x+{\\color{blue}{b}},$ where $\\color{red}{m}$ is the slope and $\\color{blue}{b}$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFor the equation $y=2x-9,$ we see that the slope is ${\\color{red}{m=2}}$ and the $y$-intercept is ${\\color{blue}{b=-9}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8e10b2d-cad1-5d50-b9fc-917c3367f814" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Graphed Line (Positive Slope)"}] :question/id #uuid "36e13cef-f61a-53e2-8f0e-9ff567dc6fb6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12852" :question/problem "![](images/46/46a04d55edd836050d30a0d4d52c4c441619babd79f93f3540f61a5f437a1ea7.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(0,-2)$ and $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-2).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b = -2.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,-2)$ and $(2,2),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-(-2)}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m = 2$ and the $y$-intercept $b = -2$ into the slope-intercept form $y = mx + b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny = 2x - 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100595 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4762cb71-f6b6-5d32-8d0b-a457db66ff97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points: Y-Intercept Given"}] :question/id #uuid "8351e1cb-c602-5968-be5a-9c337ad977de" :question/math-academy-id "e-12853" :question/problem "Calculate the equation of the straight line that passes through the points $(0,8)$ and $(3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-8}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,{\\color{blue}{b}}).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,{\\color{blue}{8}}),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b={\\color{blue}{8}}.$ \n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=8$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-2x+8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ac060cb-0c76-5050-9156-e5f17ad26fbb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Graphed Line (Negative Slope)"}] :question/id #uuid "206b8f86-4184-50fa-9999-08700d10dc4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-20867" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line shown below.\n\n![](images/42/4298ec9c12555fd5e87265617ee274ef0fa859decd3ccd9473dfe8f778492df0.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=2.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,2)$ and $(6,0),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2}{6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=2$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x+2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3833c1-4ec5-5681-ae69-6a9cceece871" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Slope and Y-Intercept From the Equation of a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a03be06-25d6-5b6e-923f-0f94ffb16319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The slope is $-\\frac{3}{5}$ and the $y$-intercept is $-10.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491474} {:answer/id #uuid "84e9f3a1-dc47-582c-b4ee-e592f49fdd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The slope is $-6$ and the $y$-intercept is $-10.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491683} {:answer/id #uuid "89057c11-e09b-538d-9575-07bd8f0a2536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The slope is $-6$ and the $y$-intercept is $10.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491688} {:answer/id #uuid "9da73662-753b-5b9d-bdfa-7aaa437822df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The slope is $\\frac{3}{5}$ and the $y$-intercept is $-10.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491730} {:answer/id #uuid "e57747e1-a38d-589e-9035-62b04fc9a98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The slope is $6$ and the $y$-intercept is $-10.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84e9f3a1-dc47-582c-b4ee-e592f49fdd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The slope is $-6$ and the $y$-intercept is $-10.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491683} :answer-field/id #uuid "d971d8d7-d07d-5bfb-82bd-ea331a02f49c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8819403-c513-52d3-9b76-6039d6a310cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-163" :question/problem "What are the slope and $y$-intercept of the line $y=-6x-10?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is in slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFor the equation $y=-6x-10,$ we see that the slope is $m=-6$ and the $y$-intercept is $b=-10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8e10b2d-cad1-5d50-b9fc-917c3367f814" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Graphed Line (Positive Slope)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f07d12ce-94ce-5d54-bec9-7fe71865524f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f07d12ce-94ce-5d54-bec9-7fe71865524f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491866} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b32c379-8d01-513f-93c8-ff1502ff40bc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e48611c-9bfd-5ce0-8c8f-1ec819016abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e48611c-9bfd-5ce0-8c8f-1ec819016abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491547} :answer-field/id #uuid "c169414f-3cfe-5352-aa9a-130012179527" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd8e8dcc-df2a-5702-b7e5-ec1a6ace2341" :question/math-academy-id "q-171704" :question/problem "![](images/73/73ba24f5e6d6f8d9b5da5c4bc4fe2912021ee32524c5c682f2e0269ac2651311.png)\n\nConsider the line shown above, which has a slope of $2.$ The equation of the line is $y=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ We know that the slope is $m=2,$ so we just need to work out $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line passes through the point $(1,5).$ Substituting this into $y=mx+b$ with $m=2$ and solving for $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+b \\\\ 5 & =2(1)+b \\\\ 5 & =2+b \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=2x+3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ac060cb-0c76-5050-9156-e5f17ad26fbb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Graphed Line (Negative Slope)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dc6d914-fd39-5623-92a1-78d84d6d0d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dc6d914-fd39-5623-92a1-78d84d6d0d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491650} :answer-field/id #uuid "40950c7b-6a6f-51fa-b7a1-3cb9ed660068" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07094bec-280a-5ee6-abd1-48ad52671389" :question/math-academy-id "q-175545" :question/problem "![](images/46/460a2471128c8f934de890ad20001c109adba3ce6fda75de211916f94d73cc57.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(-2,1)$ and $(0,-3).$\n\nThe equation of the line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-3).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=-3.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,-3)$ and $(-2,1),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-3)}{-2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=-3$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-2x-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3833c1-4ec5-5681-ae69-6a9cceece871" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Slope and Y-Intercept From the Equation of a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2132b688-06a5-555f-a09f-e4599c007b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2132b688-06a5-555f-a09f-e4599c007b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491523} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e39b8e-8a93-5b81-951f-2a77160e427d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12dfe335-8580-5766-9979-cf959b77696e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12dfe335-8580-5766-9979-cf959b77696e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491493} :answer-field/id #uuid "1877d866-099b-5758-878f-238680ec5170" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5c0d08f-6c2d-5f1f-a9ae-394015398e04" :question/math-academy-id "q-178546" :question/problem "Determine the slope and $y$-intercept of the line $y=6x+1.$\n\nThe slope of the line is\n{{field-1}}\n and the $y$-intercept is\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The equation is in slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFor the equation $y=6x+1,$ we see that the slope is $m=6$ and the $y$-intercept is $b=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8e10b2d-cad1-5d50-b9fc-917c3367f814" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Graphed Line (Positive Slope)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7b8f40-c537-5e8e-859e-acc1c60a1ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d7b8f40-c537-5e8e-859e-acc1c60a1ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491513} :answer-field/id #uuid "9faaedc2-4a9b-562e-98d9-4659b2f5c9d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "676e490f-5045-547e-8d6d-b73383592eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-178555" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dfe3b6c14454f1ca0339f1f3328cb53fe6b8d3e151b0ac7f4562aae17469bf6.png)\n\nGiven that the slope of the line shown above is $5,$ the equation of the line is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ We know that the slope is $m=5,$ so we just need to work out $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-6).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=-6.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=5$ and the $y$-intercept $b=-6$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=5x-6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ac060cb-0c76-5050-9156-e5f17ad26fbb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Graphed Line (Negative Slope)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cea09ec4-0832-5a5f-950b-c95c11bc45ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea09ec4-0832-5a5f-950b-c95c11bc45ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491804} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1164b8-c7c4-5a19-a0e9-eb886511f21c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e89aa872-b3ab-54a9-b84c-d1f28d2bc0e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-178575" :question/problem "![](images/97/97bd0e97b1927d27a4d39b5749c97f2a015af87b35780c37fa6042e9a76a209c.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(-4,2)$ and $(0,1).$\n\nThe equation of the line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,1).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=1.$ \n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(-4,2)$ and $(0,1),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2}{0-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=1$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4}x+1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4762cb71-f6b6-5d32-8d0b-a457db66ff97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points: Y-Intercept Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3d5187-7d63-5c39-924a-717a0dba4404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491481} {:answer/id #uuid "16e97e79-5e7f-520e-8023-8e914126a4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491500} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8c5b8d-4087-5738-ba0b-054362d46faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491674} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2cee86-f39a-5681-b975-f6de583d3b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x+26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491729} {:answer/id #uuid "e4337068-d71a-535f-90d6-1745c9faa29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16e97e79-5e7f-520e-8023-8e914126a4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491500} :answer-field/id #uuid "f85d5513-b0e5-547b-9aab-8a3c4a7560ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51cb6080-077c-56de-a53e-403e56ffa583" :question/math-academy-id "q-6900" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0,10)$ and $(4,26).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{26-10}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{4} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,10),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=10.$ \n\nSubstituting the slope $m=4$ and the $y$-intercept $b=10$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=4x+10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4762cb71-f6b6-5d32-8d0b-a457db66ff97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points: Y-Intercept Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd658b9-2754-5dd7-a9b8-3d4f2d2e6884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491484} {:answer/id #uuid "168d72bf-97b3-58ac-8443-80eb266860ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491499} {:answer/id #uuid "485b437d-7e75-5cce-8837-8f32097748dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491589} {:answer/id #uuid "69497a21-0cd0-5163-9969-8d36c2baacab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491643} {:answer/id #uuid "edbafbf9-0cf1-5ea9-b87a-cb8250379cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "168d72bf-97b3-58ac-8443-80eb266860ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491499} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccbd18be-40a6-50c0-bf68-210285b08443" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e261b9e8-b30d-532e-8bf2-37526414dfd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72107" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0,-1)$ and $(3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-(-1)}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,-1),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=-1.$  \n\nSubstituting the slope $m=2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=-1$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=2x-1.\n$$"}]}
